  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-16a583782062211c": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-239",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Israelites were commanded by their law to be merciful to the poor. The corners of the fields were not to be reaped (Lev. 19:9; Lev. 23:22). If a sheaf should be accidentally left in the field it was to be allowed to remain there (Deut. 24:19). This grain in the corners, and t…"
      },
      "c-679686193b8c4ec2": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-242",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This is still done by the gleaners at the close of their day’s work, sticks or stones being used as convenient though rude instruments for threshing the grain they have gathered."
      },
      "c-d6f74a42dcaf4560": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-240",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "These salutations are heard at this day in the East. The Psalmist prays that the haters of Zion may be like grass upon the house tops, and not like the grain which is reaped in the harvest field amid these mutual benedictions of employer and laborer (Psa. 129:6-8)."
      },
      "c-f8dcd7d348847107": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-241",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "In Turkey grape juice is boiled from four to five hours, until it is reduced to one fourth the quantity put in. This is called Nardenk. It is of a dark color, has an agreeable sour-sweet taste, is turbid, and not intoxicating. It is sometimes used in the manner in which the chome…"
      }
    },
